    "result_text": "The change is complete and all tests pass (BUILD SUCCESS, 1/1 tests green).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe telephone normalization already enforced a general 8\u201315 digit E.164 rule, but didn't check the national-number length per country. I added that in `OwnerTelephones.toE164(...)`, the single source of truth shared by both the normalize-on-create step and the duplicate check.\n\n**`OwnerTelephones.java`** \u2014 new `hasValidNationalLength(digits)` check applied after the 8\u201315 bound:\n- `+61` \u2192 requires exactly **9** national digits (total 11 after `+`)\n- `+1` \u2192 requires exactly **10** national digits (total 11 after `+`)\n- unrecognized country codes fall back to the general 8\u201315 rule\n\nA wrong length returns `null`, which `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` turns into `InvalidTelephoneException` \u2192 **400**.\n\n**`InvalidTelephoneException.java`** \u2014 updated the message/Javadoc to describe the per-country rule.\n\nThe new test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123` \u2192 only 5 national digits) now rejects with 400, and the base payload's `uniqueTelephone()` (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 9 national digits under `+61`) stays valid across the whole rule chain, so existing behavior is preserved.",
