    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe length check lands in the right unit. `OwnerTelephones` is already the single home for telephone normalization, so extending it with the national-number rule keeps all the E.164 knowledge in one place rather than scattering it into a new class or into the function pipeline.\n\n- The new logic is factored into a focused private helper `hasValidNationalLength(...)` with a clear name and intent-revealing Javadoc, rather than being inlined into `toE164`. `toE164` stays a readable sequence of guard clauses (`< 8 || > 15`, then `!hasValidNationalLength`), which reads naturally.\n- Expressing the rule as `digits.length() - 2 == 9` / `digits.length() - 1 == 10` (national digits) mirrors the spec's wording precisely, so a maintainer reads the code and the spec the same way. The \"unrecognized country code falls back to the general rule\" behavior is stated in the Javadoc, so the `return true` default isn't a silent surprise.\n- For two country codes an `if`-chain is the idiomatic, right-sized choice; a lookup table would be premature. Nothing is duplicated \u2014 the country-code facts restated in the exception message and Javadocs are documentation, not logic.\n- The exception message and both Javadocs were updated in step with the behavior change, so the class contracts stay honest. No class or method is turning into a catch-all.",
